1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
They are the powerful engine of the Earth's energy.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:06,000
If you fell in there, you could get boiled alive.

3
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:08,000
They support the tallest skyscrapers

4
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
and are the foundation for over 40 million miles of roads.

5
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:15,000
All the construction has been based on using crushed stones

6
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:18,000
from the early Roman roads to today's interstates.

7
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Whether they're billions of years old or as young as this morning,

8
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:25,000
they hold the secrets of the universe.

9
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:27,000
There's no way to put a price on them.

10
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:30,300
You'll never think of that pebble in your shoe the same way again.

11
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:36,240
Now, get ready to rock on Modern Marvel's Rock's Greatest Hits.

12
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:53,660
Rocks.

13
00:00:53,661 --> 00:00:57,400
They may be the most underappreciated objects in the natural world,

14
00:00:57,401 --> 00:01:01,520
but we'd be stone cold out of luck without them.

15
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:05,120
Besides providing us with shelter,

16
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:08,300
we extract metal from rocks to construct our machines.

17
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:12,400
Whether you're sitting in a chair made of steel

18
00:01:12,401 --> 00:01:13,740
or you're driving a car made of steel,

19
00:01:13,980 --> 00:01:15,240
that steel came from rocks.

20
00:01:17,020 --> 00:01:18,900
We take heat from them for warmth

21
00:01:18,901 --> 00:01:21,960
and precious minerals to make medicine.

22
00:01:21,961 --> 00:01:26,960
We rely on rocks to make soil to grow plants.

23
00:01:26,961 --> 00:01:30,960
At one time, we used them for weapons.

24
00:01:30,961 --> 00:01:36,960
They season our food and add sparkle and wealth to our lives.

25
00:01:36,961 --> 00:01:42,960
If you're operating a computer, the silicon chips that make up an important part of that computer come from rocks.

26
00:01:42,961 --> 00:01:47,960
The Earth is one huge ball of rock, 25,000 miles around,

27
00:01:47,961 --> 00:01:51,960
and over 4.5 billion years old.

28
00:01:51,961 --> 00:01:55,960
But a question, what are the most valuable rocks on Earth?

29
00:01:55,961 --> 00:02:00,960
They very well may be NASA's collection of lunar rocks,

30
00:02:00,961 --> 00:02:04,960
located at the Lyndon Johnson Space Center near Houston.

31
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:09,960
They're housed in a special building at the center,

32
00:02:09,961 --> 00:02:13,060
which was constructed to quarantine astronauts

33
00:02:13,061 --> 00:02:15,900
and material brought back from the moon during the Apollo missions.

34
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:20,640
The lab is off limits to the general public,

35
00:02:20,920 --> 00:02:24,200
and those who work here must observe stringent cleanliness protocols

36
00:02:24,201 --> 00:02:27,280
to protect the rocks from any form of Earth contamination.

37
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:33,200
All workers who come in contact with the rocks must wear a bunny suit.

38
00:02:35,020 --> 00:02:37,960
The suit that I'm wearing is a nylon cleanroom suit.

39
00:02:37,961 --> 00:02:42,100
The air that comes into this lab is filtered very well with very, very small HEPA filters,

40
00:02:42,260 --> 00:02:43,520
so the air stays very clean.

41
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:48,000
Well, this is the door to the vault where we keep our lunar samples safe.

42
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:51,380
This is equivalent to a Federal Reserve Bank vault,

43
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,600
and it's a very, very secure kind of storage.

44
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:57,980
This is a very substantial door, as you will see.

45
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:03,260
And inside here, we keep the samples that are still pristine.

46
00:03:03,261 --> 00:03:05,560
We originally brought back 842 pounds,

47
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:07,860
And you can see we have cabinets in here,

48
00:03:07,940 --> 00:03:10,200
and these cabinets have nitrogen gas running through them.

49
00:03:10,740 --> 00:03:14,280
The nitrogen protects the rocks from certain elements in the Earth's atmosphere.

50
00:03:14,620 --> 00:03:17,360
On the moon, there is no oxygen and there is no water.

51
00:03:18,060 --> 00:03:22,300
The minute the lunar samples were to come in contact with oxygen or water in our atmosphere,

52
00:03:22,301 --> 00:03:25,880
they would begin to oxidize, or in simple terms, they would begin to rust.

53
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:30,500
And the samples in a few decades wouldn't be any good for scientific study.

54
00:03:32,880 --> 00:03:35,180
Collecting these geological samples from the moon

55
00:03:35,181 --> 00:03:37,480
was a top priority of the Apollo missions.

56
00:03:39,820 --> 00:03:43,400
No better clues exist about how the moon formed and evolved.

57
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:46,620
To gather the moon rocks,

58
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:49,820
the astronauts came equipped with custom-designed tools.

59
00:03:51,260 --> 00:03:52,760
These are tongs.

60
00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:56,780
They worked by squeezing the handle, and the tongs would open.

61
00:03:56,781 --> 00:04:00,660
And this enabled the astronauts to pick up rocks off the ground

62
00:04:00,661 --> 00:04:03,520
because they really couldn't bend over in their spacesuits.

63
00:04:05,180 --> 00:04:07,680
Some of the rocks the astronauts brought back from the moon

64
00:04:07,681 --> 00:04:09,720
were similar to those found on Earth.

65
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:13,420
Many were basalt, a product of volcanic activity.

66
00:04:14,340 --> 00:04:17,020
There were lavas and there were crustal rocks,

67
00:04:17,140 --> 00:04:19,840
like the kinds of rocks we made granite tombstones out of.

68
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:23,480
As the crewmen gathered the rocks on the lunar surface,

69
00:04:24,340 --> 00:04:27,360
other rocks, tiny ones, hurtling through space,

70
00:04:27,361 --> 00:04:29,440
added an element of danger to their mission.

71
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:34,700
Such rocky debris, including meteorites, also pelts the Earth.

72
00:04:35,180 --> 00:04:39,500
But Earth's atmosphere protects us by disintegrating them or slowing them down.

73
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:44,120
The moon, which has no atmosphere, exposed the astronauts to the threat.

74
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:48,440
They come in at such fast speeds, many times the speed of a bullet.

75
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:53,420
And the spacesuits were made in such a way that they could withstand some of these impacts

76
00:04:53,421 --> 00:04:56,280
because the mass of these particles is very small.

77
00:04:57,820 --> 00:05:01,760
Despite the danger, none of the Apollo astronauts were injured by the particles.

78
00:05:01,761 --> 00:05:10,000
Back on Earth, scientists believed that the rocks recovered from the moon posed an entirely

79
00:05:10,001 --> 00:05:11,440
different kind of threat.

80
00:05:11,441 --> 00:05:16,860
We were concerned that perhaps there were bugs or some sort of Andromeda strain that

81
00:05:16,861 --> 00:05:19,360
might exist on the moon, but it was a very rare possibility.

82
00:05:19,361 --> 00:05:23,080
We understood that the radiation environment and the lack of an atmosphere on the moon

83
00:05:23,081 --> 00:05:27,400
would make it very difficult for a life to survive, but you always want to be cautious

84
00:05:27,401 --> 00:05:31,140
in an unknown environment.

85
00:05:31,141 --> 00:05:36,900
tests determined that the moon rocks contain no hint of alien life. But as

86
00:05:36,901 --> 00:05:43,500
hoped, they have helped researchers gain many new insights. Since basalt is a

87
00:05:43,501 --> 00:05:47,760
common rock on both the moon and on Earth, studying its chemistry was the

88
00:05:47,761 --> 00:05:53,340
basis for a mind-boggling theory on how the moon itself was formed over 4.5

89
00:05:53,341 --> 00:05:56,380
billion years ago.

90
00:05:56,381 --> 00:06:09,026
The leading theory right now for the formation of the moon is that very early in solar system history, a planet or a protoplanet the size of Mars impacted the very

91
00:06:09,027 --> 00:06:10,500
early planet Earth.

92
00:06:10,501 --> 00:06:13,480
The Mars-sized planet was shattered.

93
00:06:13,820 --> 00:06:17,740
The core of that Mars-sized body became part of Earth,

94
00:06:17,920 --> 00:06:22,220
and the exterior parts, the crust and the mantle,

95
00:06:22,460 --> 00:06:26,900
were all pulverized, and all those particles went into orbit around the Earth.

96
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:30,740
So, for a while, the Earth had a ring system.

97
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:35,500
And then over time, those particles began to slow down and coalesce,

98
00:06:36,260 --> 00:06:39,720
and after a while, they had all clumped up, and they became the Moon.

99
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:45,000
But what about the six sextillion tons of rock we call planet Earth?

100
00:06:45,580 --> 00:06:50,360
By the way, that's almost 800 billion tons of rock for every person on the planet.

101
00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:53,180
What are they?

102
00:06:54,580 --> 00:06:58,760
In simple terms, rocks are composed of one or more minerals.

103
00:06:59,840 --> 00:07:02,300
Minerals are the most solid material found on Earth.

104
00:07:02,840 --> 00:07:04,960
And they always have the same chemical makeup.

105
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:09,080
There are three basic classifications of rock.

106
00:07:10,040 --> 00:07:14,160
One is igneous, like the rocks found in the lava fields of the Hawaiian Islands.

107
00:07:15,680 --> 00:07:19,100
An igneous rock is a rock that's formed from cooled magma,

108
00:07:19,400 --> 00:07:24,380
magma being liquid molten rock that has come to the surface or near the surface like you would see in a volcano.

109
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:30,740
Another type of rock is sedimentary, like that found in the Grand Canyon.

110
00:07:31,420 --> 00:07:35,920
Sedimentary rocks are formed by erosion, making bigger rocks into smaller rocks.

111
00:07:35,921 --> 00:07:39,400
And these smaller rocks, when they lay on top of each other over many, many years,

112
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:42,820
they cement together until they form a solid rock, a sedimentary rock.

113
00:07:44,720 --> 00:07:47,000
The third type of rock is metamorphic.

114
00:07:47,940 --> 00:07:52,600
Metamorphic rock forms when a pre-existing rock type is subjected to heat and extreme pressure.

115
00:07:55,220 --> 00:07:58,200
This causes a physical or chemical change in the rock.

116
00:07:59,100 --> 00:08:04,060
It could be an igneous rock originally, sedimentary rock originally, or another metamorphic rock.

117
00:08:04,061 --> 00:08:07,580
The word metamorphic, meta means change, morph means form.

118
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:09,820
So in some fashion it has changed in form,

119
00:08:09,920 --> 00:08:13,520
either through a change in the mineralogy or the hardness of the rock.

120
00:08:14,640 --> 00:08:18,640
It can take millions of years for a rock to morph from one form to another.

121
00:08:19,560 --> 00:08:22,280
Yet in our never-ending drive to put rocks to use,

122
00:08:22,580 --> 00:08:24,920
we're speeding up the process with technology.

123
00:08:26,680 --> 00:08:28,840
Sometimes we can almost do the impossible.

124
00:08:30,260 --> 00:08:32,320
You ever hear the phrase, sink like a rock?

125
00:08:34,060 --> 00:08:37,120
Well, with today's technology, we can reverse that.

126
00:08:37,440 --> 00:08:40,340
We get some products that actually weigh less than water.

127
00:08:40,840 --> 00:08:42,960
They'll actually float when you put them in water.

128
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:47,980
This is lightweight aggregate, but you won't find it in nature.

129
00:08:50,200 --> 00:08:55,040
This rock has been manufactured at the Stalite Company in Gold Hill, North Carolina.

130
00:08:58,560 --> 00:09:01,100
Composed of sand, gravel, and crushed stone,

131
00:09:01,101 --> 00:09:05,100
Aggregate is a primary ingredient in concrete.

132
00:09:05,101 --> 00:09:09,100
Without concrete, we wouldn't lay foundations strong enough

133
00:09:09,101 --> 00:09:12,100
for buildings to scrape the sky,

134
00:09:12,101 --> 00:09:14,100
or build titanic dams,

135
00:09:14,101 --> 00:09:18,100
or pave the sidewalks leading to our homes.

136
00:09:20,100 --> 00:09:24,100
However, all aggregates are not created equal.

137
00:09:24,101 --> 00:09:29,100
Lightweight aggregate composed of such light but strong rocks,

138
00:09:29,101 --> 00:09:33,580
Esmeta argillite can make much lighter weight concrete than traditional aggregate.

139
00:09:35,420 --> 00:09:39,960
And lightweight concrete is desirable because it can reduce construction costs.

140
00:09:41,400 --> 00:09:45,680
Lightweight aggregate reduces the weight of the concrete by 25 to 30 percent,

141
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:51,740
which allows you to use a lot less foundation, less reinforcing steels,

142
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:55,620
and there's less seismic mass to the foundations as well.

143
00:09:55,621 --> 00:10:01,720
The rocks that the Stalight Company uses to produce the lightweight aggregate come from

144
00:10:01,721 --> 00:10:08,340
North Carolina's Gold Hill Quarry, operated by the Vulcan Materials Company.

145
00:10:08,341 --> 00:10:09,340
This is our meta-argillite.

146
00:10:09,341 --> 00:10:12,840
It is not a slate, actually, but it has a slatey appearance.

147
00:10:12,841 --> 00:10:15,140
It's very hard.

148
00:10:15,141 --> 00:10:19,380
This rock is so hard, it has to be blasted out of the ground.

149
00:10:19,381 --> 00:10:24,700
The explosive used is made of ammonium nitrate, an ingredient also found in yard fertilizers.

150
00:10:24,701 --> 00:10:30,700
The explosives are placed in a pattern that will create a domino effect when detonated.

151
00:10:30,701 --> 00:10:33,700
These holes are approximately 46 feet deep.

152
00:10:33,701 --> 00:10:37,700
We drill on a 15 by 17 foot pattern.

153
00:10:37,701 --> 00:10:39,700
There's a booster.

154
00:10:39,701 --> 00:10:41,700
Inside the booster will go a cap.

155
00:10:41,701 --> 00:10:43,700
The cap is a non-electric cap.

156
00:10:43,701 --> 00:10:48,700
It is set off by a powder substance inside the tube.

157
00:10:48,701 --> 00:10:58,200
The amount of rock that's blasted can vary, but here we usually get about 30,000 tons

158
00:10:58,201 --> 00:11:00,200
out of a shot.

159
00:11:00,201 --> 00:11:02,700
First stop for these rocks, the rock crusher.

160
00:11:02,701 --> 00:11:06,820
The rock is dumped onto a feeder.

161
00:11:06,821 --> 00:11:12,700
This feeder is fastened to a set of something called drizzly bars, and they are like grates

162
00:11:12,701 --> 00:11:14,200
with openings between them.

163
00:11:14,201 --> 00:11:16,260
These things will feed the rock forward.

164
00:11:16,261 --> 00:11:22,020
It allows the smaller rock to fall out and not go through the primary crusher, therefore saving energy.

165
00:11:24,200 --> 00:11:27,360
Up to 7,000 tons of rock are crushed each day.

166
00:11:28,400 --> 00:11:32,380
That's the weight of a fleet of almost 4,000 mid-sized automobiles.

167
00:11:35,320 --> 00:11:39,160
After crushing, the rocks tumble through several screens to be sorted

168
00:11:39,161 --> 00:11:43,180
and are then sent to a rotary kiln.

169
00:11:43,181 --> 00:11:48,320
The kiln is where modern alchemy turns heavy rock into light,

170
00:11:49,040 --> 00:11:53,420
for it is here that the rock material expands under heat without losing strength.

171
00:11:53,880 --> 00:11:57,460
And as it slowly tumbles through the kiln, the temperature slowly rises

172
00:11:57,461 --> 00:12:00,460
up to about 2,100 degrees Fahrenheit,

173
00:12:00,800 --> 00:12:03,920
and at 2,100 degrees, the material is actually starting to soften,

174
00:12:04,340 --> 00:12:08,720
and then the gas is inside, basically sulfur dioxide and some other gases form,

175
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:09,760
and they try to escape.

176
00:12:09,761 --> 00:12:11,560
And what they do is they create millions

177
00:12:11,660 --> 00:12:14,760
of little non-connected cells, millions of little air bubbles

178
00:12:14,860 --> 00:12:18,260
that are trapped inside the aggregate.

179
00:12:18,360 --> 00:12:20,500
Then the material falls and goes to the cooler,

180
00:12:20,600 --> 00:12:21,600
and it hardens.

181
00:12:21,700 --> 00:12:23,000
That's how it gets its low weight,

182
00:12:23,100 --> 00:12:24,800
because none of the cells are actually connected,

183
00:12:24,900 --> 00:12:28,320
but there's millions of them trapped inside the aggregate.

184
00:12:28,420 --> 00:12:30,320
Stay Light then sells the cooled aggregate

185
00:12:30,420 --> 00:12:33,020
to construction firms all over the world

186
00:12:33,120 --> 00:12:38,620
to make lightweight concrete used in skyscrapers, bridges,

187
00:12:38,621 --> 00:12:40,920
and other major construction projects.

188
00:12:47,020 --> 00:12:50,200
One type of rock dominates our city landscapes.

189
00:12:51,340 --> 00:12:52,660
Carved from nature's majesty

190
00:12:52,661 --> 00:12:56,200
into finely cut building blocks of countless classic structures,

191
00:12:58,900 --> 00:13:02,020
granite is synonymous with hardness and durability.

192
00:13:03,420 --> 00:13:05,300
You can count on it to last,

193
00:13:05,760 --> 00:13:07,640
from the facade of the Empire State Building

194
00:13:07,641 --> 00:13:09,920
to your glistening kitchen countertop.

195
00:13:11,920 --> 00:13:14,860
And it all comes from quarries like the Rock of Ages

196
00:13:14,861 --> 00:13:16,240
in Barrie, Vermont.

197
00:13:17,960 --> 00:13:19,920
Over 500 feet deep,

198
00:13:20,460 --> 00:13:22,620
the quarry is one of the largest in the world,

199
00:13:23,860 --> 00:13:25,900
noted for both the quality of its rock

200
00:13:25,901 --> 00:13:27,840
and the extent of a deposit.

201
00:13:28,840 --> 00:13:32,180
Barrie stone itself is just an exceptional granite,

202
00:13:32,280 --> 00:13:33,880
probably the finest gray granite

203
00:13:33,881 --> 00:13:35,520
yet discovered anywhere in the world.

204
00:13:36,480 --> 00:13:38,800
The deposit's been measured by sound technology.

205
00:13:39,320 --> 00:13:42,740
It's approximately four miles long, one and a half to two miles wide,

206
00:13:42,800 --> 00:13:46,120
and it's estimated to be up to about ten miles in thickness.

207
00:13:47,480 --> 00:13:52,080
That's a tower of granite the height of over 36 Empire State Buildings.

208
00:13:54,680 --> 00:13:59,120
The deposit at the Rock of Ages, like all granite, is igneous rock.

209
00:13:59,121 --> 00:14:07,100
It formed from magma generated millions of years ago by friction between tectonic plates deep below the surface.

210
00:14:08,580 --> 00:14:17,700
Less dense than the solid rock surrounding it, the molten material rose up through the cracks in the overlying rock and cooled into the huge granite deposit.

211
00:14:22,820 --> 00:14:27,940
Granite from the Rock of Ages quarry has been used in many of America's greatest buildings and monuments.

212
00:14:27,941 --> 00:14:34,420
We were very, very proud to be a part of the fabrication of the National World War II Memorial

213
00:14:34,421 --> 00:14:37,440
on the Mall in Washington, D.C.

214
00:14:37,441 --> 00:14:41,956
The steps of the Capitol Building in Washington, D.C.

215
00:14:41,957 --> 00:14:45,620
are also fabricated from very gray granite.

216
00:14:45,621 --> 00:14:51,620
Rocks such as granite and marble are often used as so-called dimension stones.

217
00:14:51,621 --> 00:14:57,180
The term dimension stone refers to stone that's cut to be a certain dimensional size rather

218
00:14:57,181 --> 00:15:01,680
than aggregate that's to be used for crushed stone and other purposes.

219
00:15:01,681 --> 00:15:10,060
Often it's taken out of the ground in giant blocks, weighing as much as 200 tons.

220
00:15:10,061 --> 00:15:17,740
Because granite is so hard, it takes giant powerful drills and saws to cut into it.

221
00:15:17,741 --> 00:15:23,360
Quarrymen call the process of separating the granite into blocks, channeling.

222
00:15:23,361 --> 00:15:27,360
To separate a block from the quarry wall, they first have to cut around the sides and

223
00:15:27,361 --> 00:15:31,360
the back of the block.

224
00:15:31,361 --> 00:15:33,360
One method uses a slot drill.

225
00:15:33,361 --> 00:15:37,220
A slot drill is an air-driven rotary drill.

226
00:15:37,221 --> 00:15:41,720
It is set up so that it drills a vertical hole up to about 20 feet in depth.

227
00:15:41,721 --> 00:15:45,020
Then the drill rod retracts automatically, moves over on a tracking mechanism.

228
00:15:45,021 --> 00:15:50,520
We sink another hole in another until we have a row of closely spaced holes up one side,

229
00:15:50,521 --> 00:15:53,020
across the back and down the other side.

230
00:15:54,500 --> 00:15:57,100
Once workers drill these initial sets of holes,

231
00:15:57,101 --> 00:15:58,600
they make another pass at the rock

232
00:15:58,601 --> 00:16:00,900
drilling out the granite between the holes,

233
00:16:00,901 --> 00:16:02,100
called the web.

234
00:16:05,640 --> 00:16:07,660
But now comes the hard part.

235
00:16:07,661 --> 00:16:09,080
They've got to separate the bottom

236
00:16:09,081 --> 00:16:10,700
without destroying the equipment.

237
00:16:12,800 --> 00:16:15,120
The process is called undercutting.

238
00:16:15,121 --> 00:16:17,220
It begins by drilling a series of holes

239
00:16:17,221 --> 00:16:18,740
in the bottom of the block

240
00:16:18,741 --> 00:16:20,940
and will end with a huge explosion.

241
00:16:22,480 --> 00:16:25,740
We use primer cord that's often used as a fuse

242
00:16:25,741 --> 00:16:26,820
in other industries.

243
00:16:26,821 --> 00:16:28,420
Looks like a giant jump rope.

244
00:16:28,421 --> 00:16:30,420
It's on a large reel like a wire.

245
00:16:30,421 --> 00:16:33,220
It's reamed into the holes with a metal rod,

246
00:16:33,221 --> 00:16:34,420
about every other hole.

247
00:16:34,421 --> 00:16:36,660
Then it's tied together electrically

248
00:16:36,661 --> 00:16:38,420
and set off remotely.

249
00:16:39,700 --> 00:16:40,860
Fire!

250
00:16:44,360 --> 00:16:46,240
Once loosened, the slabs are lifted

251
00:16:46,241 --> 00:16:48,560
to the rim of the quarry by giant derricks,

252
00:16:48,561 --> 00:16:49,261
cranes.

253
00:16:49,262 --> 00:16:55,920
The most powerful of the derricks can lift an astounding 200 tons of stone at a time

254
00:16:55,921 --> 00:16:58,797
out of the 500 foot deep quarry.

255
00:16:58,798 --> 00:17:04,280
We lower cable from a derrick and put it in a loop around the

256
00:17:04,281 --> 00:17:05,209
perimeter of the stone.

257
00:17:05,210 --> 00:17:08,240
We don't go underneath the stone because we would have no way of lifting it

258
00:17:08,241 --> 00:17:09,849
up to put the cable underneath it.

259
00:17:09,850 --> 00:17:13,020
So we go around the perimeter, actually cut a small notch into each

260
00:17:13,021 --> 00:17:17,760
of the four corners of the stone and draw the cable tightly just like a slip knot so that the

261
00:17:17,761 --> 00:17:21,000
harder that the block pulls on the cable, the tighter it becomes.

262
00:17:21,700 --> 00:17:25,200
Next, the granite goes to a processing plant to be cut and polished.

263
00:17:27,580 --> 00:17:32,640
This is where workers craft it into the dimension stones used for our buildings and homes.

264
00:17:37,520 --> 00:17:42,540
The most unusual place the granite might show up is six feet over your head.

265
00:17:42,541 --> 00:17:48,400
Craftsmen first work out tombstone design and lettering on a computer.

266
00:17:49,120 --> 00:17:55,960
Then that design is transferred from paper to a rubber sheet by the computer.

267
00:17:57,820 --> 00:18:02,400
The rubber sheet is then temporarily adhered or glued to the surface of the granite,

268
00:18:02,760 --> 00:18:05,520
and parts of it are cut away to form a stencil.

269
00:18:05,521 --> 00:18:14,520
A sandblaster then takes over, spewing its abrasive under high pressure at 125 pounds per square inch.

270
00:18:15,660 --> 00:18:17,900
Even granite gives way under this assault.

271
00:18:18,880 --> 00:18:26,900
The abrasive actually will hit the rubber, but because it can absorb some of the energy, it deflects and it bounces away.

272
00:18:28,120 --> 00:18:32,720
While granite earns its reputation as a tough and versatile player above and below ground,

273
00:18:33,520 --> 00:18:37,120
Some rocks are in demand for their durability and beauty.

274
00:18:39,560 --> 00:18:42,900
It's been a favorite of artists and architects for centuries,

275
00:18:43,480 --> 00:18:47,120
whether used in great works of art like Michelangelo's Statue of David

276
00:18:47,121 --> 00:18:51,320
or classic buildings like the United States Capitol and the Lincoln Memorial.

277
00:18:52,280 --> 00:18:55,080
Marble is one of the most sought-after materials on the planet

278
00:18:55,081 --> 00:18:58,820
and built a global industry worth more than $50 billion.

279
00:18:58,821 --> 00:19:00,920
$1,000.

280
00:19:00,921 --> 00:19:03,320
MARBLE IS SUCH DESIRABLE STONE

281
00:19:03,321 --> 00:19:07,620
BECAUSE UNIFIES TWO VERY IMPORTANT THINGS,

282
00:19:07,621 --> 00:19:11,760
THE BEAUTY AND THE STRENGTH.

283
00:19:11,761 --> 00:19:14,460
Most marble quarries are above ground,

284
00:19:14,461 --> 00:19:17,880
but the Vermont Marble Company's mine in Danby, Vermont,

285
00:19:17,881 --> 00:19:20,540
is the largest underground marble quarry in the world.

286
00:19:23,420 --> 00:19:26,020
Their marble supply here reaches over one mile

287
00:19:26,021 --> 00:19:29,140
into the earth and is spread over 25 acres.

288
00:19:32,420 --> 00:19:34,700
Marble is a metamorphic rock formed

289
00:19:34,701 --> 00:19:37,180
by the alteration of limestone or dolomite.

290
00:19:40,480 --> 00:19:45,580
It's so hard, they use diamond wire saws to cut it.

291
00:19:45,581 --> 00:19:47,680
Diamonds are the hardest of all rocks

292
00:19:47,681 --> 00:19:52,260
and one of the few strong enough to cut through marble.

293
00:19:52,261 --> 00:19:55,340
The diamonds are strung on a flexible wire.

294
00:19:55,341 --> 00:20:00,240
We put it on a certain sequence, and we start with a spring, and we slide it on the cable.

295
00:20:01,180 --> 00:20:09,380
And then we use a spacer, and then we use a pearl, what we call pearl because it's round and it's expensive, and it's got diamonds.

296
00:20:09,960 --> 00:20:16,020
And we do another spacer, a spring, spacer, a pearl.

297
00:20:19,160 --> 00:20:25,040
When the diamond saws blur into motion, the workers keep their distance in case the wire breaks.

298
00:20:25,340 --> 00:20:27,840
It's very dangerous work.

299
00:20:27,841 --> 00:20:30,100
We have to be careful where you're standing,

300
00:20:30,101 --> 00:20:32,940
because when the wire breaks, you could get hit with pieces

301
00:20:32,941 --> 00:20:37,180
coming off the wire, moving at a high rate of speed.

302
00:20:37,181 --> 00:20:40,720
The workers select only the highest grade of stone.

303
00:20:40,721 --> 00:20:43,720
What you're looking at here is the face of the gallery side

304
00:20:43,721 --> 00:20:44,720
area.

305
00:20:44,721 --> 00:20:48,000
And that black and gray and brown streak you see in there

306
00:20:48,001 --> 00:20:49,500
is what we call tunnel rock.

307
00:20:49,501 --> 00:20:52,460
It's not the desirable stuff that we're after.

308
00:20:52,461 --> 00:20:54,980
This is actually the stuff that we desire.

309
00:20:54,981 --> 00:20:58,200
This particular block right here is what we call an imperial marble.

310
00:20:58,460 --> 00:21:01,300
It's some of the finest marble in the world.

311
00:21:02,780 --> 00:21:07,120
Pure white marble is the result of the metamorphism of very pure limestone.

312
00:21:09,740 --> 00:21:12,260
When mineral impurities are present in the limestone,

313
00:21:12,261 --> 00:21:16,980
they can produce the characteristic swirls and veins in many varieties of colored marble.

314
00:21:16,981 --> 00:21:25,640
blocks sliced from the wall can weigh as much as 1,000 metric tons

315
00:21:25,641 --> 00:21:29,360
and are worth about $10,000 before being processed

316
00:21:29,361 --> 00:21:34,820
workers cut them down to about 45,000 pounds

317
00:21:34,821 --> 00:21:38,380
to make them more manageable during transport to the processing plant

318
00:21:38,381 --> 00:21:41,740
it too is underground

319
00:21:41,741 --> 00:21:47,280
Here the marble is cut to the exact dimensions specified by customers.

320
00:21:47,980 --> 00:21:49,860
Then it is sent to the polisher.

321
00:21:50,860 --> 00:21:56,980
It has 14 different heads on it, and it has different abrasives that are put down to the head,

322
00:21:56,981 --> 00:22:03,780
and then the marble is fed through on a conveyor, and the heads come down, and each one does its part.

323
00:22:03,920 --> 00:22:09,640
And when it comes out the other side, you can either have what you call a hone or a glassy finish.

324
00:22:11,740 --> 00:22:17,880
In the past, the quarry stone has been ordered for both the Jefferson Memorial and the United

325
00:22:17,881 --> 00:22:22,560
States Supreme Court.

326
00:22:22,561 --> 00:22:27,080
Yet another type of rock holds the precious stuff industry uses to make everything, from

327
00:22:27,081 --> 00:22:31,780
your car to your appliances to your paper clips.

328
00:22:31,781 --> 00:22:36,860
But prying it loose requires a lot of water, heat, and noise.

329
00:22:41,740 --> 00:22:46,140
Our modern world is built on a foundation of iron.

330
00:22:47,180 --> 00:22:49,240
Mined in 50 countries for its durability,

331
00:22:50,040 --> 00:22:53,960
iron makes up approximately 95% of all metals used today.

332
00:22:54,440 --> 00:22:55,940
Iron is used to make steel.

333
00:22:56,280 --> 00:22:59,780
We would not have all of the factories, the appliances, the cars.

334
00:23:00,240 --> 00:23:03,620
None of the things that we know today in modern civilization would exist

335
00:23:03,621 --> 00:23:05,520
basically without iron and iron ore.

336
00:23:06,880 --> 00:23:09,240
And iron comes from rock like this.

337
00:23:09,241 --> 00:23:15,960
When a rock is valuable enough to be mined for the metals or minerals trapped within, it's called ore.

338
00:23:18,360 --> 00:23:21,060
Minnesota is one of the most iron-rich states.

339
00:23:21,800 --> 00:23:26,000
Minnesota was blessed with a large deposit of iron called the Biwabic Iron Formation,

340
00:23:26,360 --> 00:23:31,560
and it extends for about 110 miles long from Babbitt, Minnesota, down to Grand Rapids, Minnesota.

341
00:23:32,940 --> 00:23:36,520
The iron ore began forming over 2 billion years ago,

342
00:23:36,521 --> 00:23:40,120
when the area that's now Minnesota was covered by a shallow sea.

343
00:23:41,460 --> 00:23:45,720
The iron source was located to the north of the iron range

344
00:23:45,721 --> 00:23:47,900
and was from volcanic material,

345
00:23:48,500 --> 00:23:52,700
and that was deposited into a water-filled basin

346
00:23:52,701 --> 00:23:59,140
and later buried and heated and formed into a hard iron formation rock.

347
00:24:00,100 --> 00:24:01,940
Since the late 19th century,

348
00:24:01,941 --> 00:24:05,880
more than 4 billion tons of ore have been mined from the Biowabic formation.

349
00:24:06,520 --> 00:24:11,880
Iron mining began in 1892 near the town of Mount Iron, Minnesota.

350
00:24:12,380 --> 00:24:15,640
Then from that point on, more and more mining came into place.

351
00:24:16,020 --> 00:24:17,300
The initial mines were underground.

352
00:24:17,540 --> 00:24:19,300
They later turned into open pit mines.

353
00:24:20,560 --> 00:24:24,980
The most valuable iron ore during the days of Minnesota mining was hematite,

354
00:24:25,440 --> 00:24:27,460
which is nearly 60% iron.

355
00:24:29,920 --> 00:24:33,920
Within six decades, miners had exhausted the rich supply.

356
00:24:34,440 --> 00:24:37,040
It went through World War I, World War II.

357
00:24:37,520 --> 00:24:43,760
Vast quantities of iron ore were mined to provide steel for the battleships and the tanks

358
00:24:43,761 --> 00:24:46,480
and everything else that went along with those two war efforts.

359
00:24:46,760 --> 00:24:49,520
And in the process, of course, a lot of the natural iron ore,

360
00:24:49,620 --> 00:24:52,020
the stuff that you could just mine out of the ground, was exhausted.

361
00:24:53,100 --> 00:24:58,960
So in the early 1950s, a new process was developed called the taconite process.

362
00:24:58,961 --> 00:25:05,880
With about 22% iron content, taconite ore is nowhere near as rich as hematite,

363
00:25:06,380 --> 00:25:10,080
which could be loaded directly from the ground into steel-making blast furnaces.

364
00:25:14,660 --> 00:25:20,360
With taconite, the iron content must be extracted grain by grain and then concentrated.

365
00:25:21,100 --> 00:25:24,040
Key to the process is taconite's magnetic qualities.

366
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:29,640
An important characteristic of the taconite that's being mined is that the iron is magnetic,

367
00:25:29,920 --> 00:25:34,400
and it can be separated from the non-magnetic material very easily through magnetic separation.

368
00:25:34,800 --> 00:25:37,960
To illustrate that point, I'll hold a magnet onto the black material here,

369
00:25:38,400 --> 00:25:39,620
and you will see that it sticks.

370
00:25:40,180 --> 00:25:43,600
The white material, which is quartz, is non-magnetic, and the magnet falls off.

371
00:25:44,820 --> 00:25:49,700
The process of magnetic separation begins by blasting the stone from out of the ground.

372
00:25:49,701 --> 00:25:54,500
The procedure is similar to that used for blasting aggregate loose.

373
00:25:55,300 --> 00:25:59,720
Four, three, two, one, zero, fire.

374
00:26:07,720 --> 00:26:11,380
Large hull trucks carry the blasted rock to a processing plant.

375
00:26:11,880 --> 00:26:14,280
This is where we take the blasted ore.

376
00:26:14,600 --> 00:26:16,480
We dump it into this crusher.

377
00:26:16,481 --> 00:26:20,040
It's a giant gyrating cone that slowly turns.

378
00:26:20,160 --> 00:26:23,820
As it turns, it crushes the ore against the side wall,

379
00:26:24,380 --> 00:26:27,060
reduces it in size from this plastic material

380
00:26:27,061 --> 00:26:28,800
down to about minus six inches.

381
00:26:30,360 --> 00:26:33,520
From here, the process involves reducing rocks

382
00:26:33,521 --> 00:26:35,200
to smaller and smaller bits

383
00:26:35,201 --> 00:26:37,640
so the magnetic iron can be extracted.

384
00:26:39,320 --> 00:26:42,540
From the crusher, the rocks go through a series of mills

385
00:26:42,541 --> 00:26:44,160
inside the processing plant.

386
00:26:44,161 --> 00:26:46,380
So the first stage of grinding is called the rot mill,

387
00:26:46,480 --> 00:26:51,360
And what we do there is we introduce the crushed ore with water and put it into a slurry form,

388
00:26:51,540 --> 00:26:54,540
which is just a mixture of water with the ground material.

389
00:26:54,900 --> 00:27:01,280
All of our processing is done wet, so we have to mix water with whatever material we have to transport it through the process.

390
00:27:01,880 --> 00:27:04,880
And then we feed it into these large mills that rotate and tumble.

391
00:27:05,160 --> 00:27:08,240
Inside of these mills, we put large diameter grinding rods.

392
00:27:08,241 --> 00:27:10,860
These are about 4 inches in diameter and 20 feet long.

393
00:27:10,861 --> 00:27:16,460
And as these rods tumble over with the turning of the mill, they grind the ore into a finer slurry.

394
00:27:17,840 --> 00:27:20,860
Then the slurry goes through its first set of magnetic separators,

395
00:27:21,600 --> 00:27:27,300
which attract particles with at least 100 times more power than the magnet on your refrigerator at home.

396
00:27:28,160 --> 00:27:32,440
Magnetic separators are large rotating drums that have permanent magnets inside of them.

397
00:27:32,760 --> 00:27:39,100
The magnetic portion of the ground material is then picked up and separated from the non-magnetic portion.

398
00:27:39,101 --> 00:27:45,580
The particles are then sent to ball mills that grind them down to the consistency of face powder.

399
00:27:46,640 --> 00:27:50,580
We use one and a half inch diameter grinding balls, which are fed into the mill,

400
00:27:50,740 --> 00:27:53,520
and as they tumble, they grind the ore even finer.

401
00:27:55,040 --> 00:27:59,600
Not ready yet, the material then goes through another set of magnetic separators.

402
00:28:00,400 --> 00:28:04,220
It emerges as a concentrate of about 67% iron.

403
00:28:05,100 --> 00:28:10,420
At that point in time, we then start adding some limestone and dolomite back into the process

404
00:28:10,421 --> 00:28:12,440
to make a very special pellet for our customer.

405
00:28:14,200 --> 00:28:18,020
These pellets are the form in which the iron will be fed into the blast furnaces.

406
00:28:19,580 --> 00:28:22,220
But first, the concentrate must be dried.

407
00:28:22,800 --> 00:28:29,700
In that plant, we used vacuum disc dryers to actually suck the moisture out of this wet concentrate.

408
00:28:30,360 --> 00:28:32,400
An air blast loosens the particles.

409
00:28:33,020 --> 00:28:36,860
That concentrate is then fed into what we call balling disks.

410
00:28:37,120 --> 00:28:41,800
And we spin these disks and we create these pellets, which we call green balls.

411
00:28:42,800 --> 00:28:45,300
Then the pellets are baked in a giant furnace.

412
00:28:45,860 --> 00:28:52,860
And this is a long 260-foot furnace where the pellets are fired at 2,400 degrees Fahrenheit

413
00:28:52,861 --> 00:28:55,800
and then cooled down as they exit the process.

414
00:28:55,801 --> 00:28:59,280
The pellets have to be hardened to a certain strength

415
00:28:59,281 --> 00:29:01,600
in order for them to withstand the transportation

416
00:29:01,601 --> 00:29:05,420
that occurs between here and the blast furnace.

417
00:29:06,480 --> 00:29:08,960
The pellets are now over 60% iron.

418
00:29:09,540 --> 00:29:12,420
When the pellets come off the end of the furnace, they're quite hot yet,

419
00:29:12,480 --> 00:29:14,720
and when they enter into the stockpile behind us,

420
00:29:14,780 --> 00:29:16,320
they're still at a couple of hundred degrees.

421
00:29:17,560 --> 00:29:19,980
From here, the pellets are loaded into rail cars

422
00:29:19,981 --> 00:29:21,940
and shipped down to a port on Lake Superior.

423
00:29:22,480 --> 00:29:24,260
From there, the pellets are loaded into boats

424
00:29:24,261 --> 00:29:26,440
where they begin their journey down to the blast furnaces

425
00:29:26,441 --> 00:29:27,840
at the southern end of Lake Michigan.

426
00:29:29,280 --> 00:29:36,280
In the blast furnaces, the pellets are melted into molten iron.

427
00:29:36,281 --> 00:29:43,280
Some furnaces using the pellets can produce over 10,000 tons of molten iron a day.

428
00:29:43,281 --> 00:29:50,280
From here, the molten iron will go to foundries, where the steel is made to build our world.

429
00:29:50,281 --> 00:29:57,280
As rocks rich with iron demand a complex process to extract their treasure,

430
00:29:57,281 --> 00:30:02,820
Another kind of invaluable rock comes prefabricated by nature.

431
00:30:06,120 --> 00:30:09,640
When it comes to rocks, bigger doesn't always mean better.

432
00:30:10,880 --> 00:30:13,000
The smallest rocks of all, sand and gravel,

433
00:30:13,480 --> 00:30:17,460
are crucial ingredients in construction projects requiring asphalt or concrete.

434
00:30:20,000 --> 00:30:24,580
Typically asphalt is in the range of 95% stone products.

435
00:30:25,040 --> 00:30:26,740
Concrete is about 80%.

436
00:30:26,741 --> 00:30:30,940
For a single family home, it's about 400 tons of stone.

437
00:30:31,900 --> 00:30:39,220
It's estimated that 38,000 tons of aggregate are necessary to construct one mile of a four-lane interstate highway.

438
00:30:42,940 --> 00:30:49,360
Frost rock, sand and gravel and lightweight aggregate have been essential building materials since ancient times.

439
00:30:49,361 --> 00:30:58,820
From a historical perspective, you look back, basically all the construction has been based on using crushed stone, sand and gravel type products.

440
00:30:58,980 --> 00:31:08,060
From the early Roman roads to today's interstates, basically our nation and our economy are based on a solid foundation of construction aggregates.

441
00:31:09,920 --> 00:31:14,120
Unlike rock quarries where we rely on explosives to blast the aggregate loose,

442
00:31:14,121 --> 00:31:22,680
the deposits in many sand and gravel quarries come ready-made by mother nature

443
00:31:22,681 --> 00:31:27,740
they're situated where the loose rock has existed since prehistoric times

444
00:31:27,741 --> 00:31:36,460
like here in prince george county virginia about a hundred million years ago a river ran through

445
00:31:36,461 --> 00:31:41,980
this area leaving layers of rock along its shores weathering breaks the rock down into

446
00:31:41,981 --> 00:31:43,370
various size fractions.

447
00:31:43,371 --> 00:31:47,660
As they are moved along the river channels, they are rounded and broken

448
00:31:47,661 --> 00:31:49,950
into smaller and smaller sizes.

449
00:31:49,951 --> 00:31:54,900
With each passing century, the river deposited more and more layers

450
00:31:54,901 --> 00:32:01,165
of loose sand and gravel. The excavator is loading material that's not been blasted.

451
00:32:01,166 --> 00:32:02,060
It's a loose

452
00:32:02,061 --> 00:32:05,009
material that we can dig quite easily.

453
00:32:05,010 --> 00:32:09,200
Sand is composed of rocks such as feldspar, limestone,

454
00:32:09,201 --> 00:32:10,087
and quartz.

455
00:32:10,088 --> 00:32:17,180
Gravel consists of pebbles, stones, and fragments of such minerals as shale and granite.

456
00:32:17,181 --> 00:32:22,706
We mine the sand and gravel with a 5.6 yard cubic excavator.

457
00:32:22,707 --> 00:32:25,100
Our haul trucks are 40 ton

458
00:32:25,101 --> 00:32:26,131
articulated trucks.

459
00:32:26,132 --> 00:32:30,200
On a good day we can average between 8 and 10,000 tons with this operation.

460
00:32:30,201 --> 00:32:36,200
We haul the material to the surge pile, dozer pushes it over, and a loader picks it up and

461
00:32:36,201 --> 00:32:39,000
puts it into the feed hopper.

462
00:32:39,001 --> 00:32:41,220
The feed hopper distributes the sand and gravel

463
00:32:41,221 --> 00:32:44,020
onto a huge conveyor belt that transports it

464
00:32:44,021 --> 00:32:47,700
to the main processing plant.

465
00:32:47,701 --> 00:32:49,580
With the price of diesel fuel going up,

466
00:32:49,581 --> 00:32:51,700
we didn't want to have to haul the material over a mile.

467
00:32:51,701 --> 00:32:54,500
So we installed almost a mile of conveyor belt

468
00:32:54,501 --> 00:32:58,600
that will carry approximately 1,000 tons an hour.

469
00:32:58,601 --> 00:33:01,200
At the processing plant, a vibrating machine

470
00:33:01,201 --> 00:33:04,320
with a series of sifting screens separates the sand

471
00:33:04,321 --> 00:33:06,220
from the gravel.

472
00:33:06,221 --> 00:33:08,280
Once the material hits the number one screen,

473
00:33:08,281 --> 00:33:10,460
that material is then sized according

474
00:33:10,461 --> 00:33:14,500
to whether it goes into the gravel or the sand circuit.

475
00:33:14,501 --> 00:33:16,320
Each of the screens has a smaller mesh

476
00:33:16,321 --> 00:33:19,120
than the one above it.

477
00:33:19,121 --> 00:33:21,820
The larger gravel rocks stay at the top,

478
00:33:21,821 --> 00:33:25,360
and the smaller sand particles drop to the bottom.

479
00:33:25,361 --> 00:33:28,480
That begins the process of sorting the material by size.

480
00:33:28,481 --> 00:33:29,660
It's essentially like this.

481
00:33:34,320 --> 00:33:42,540
The material goes across the first screen and then goes through a series of additional

482
00:33:42,541 --> 00:33:46,880
screens and is sorted by size in decreasing diameter.

483
00:33:51,080 --> 00:33:55,500
Once separated from the gravel, the sand is sent through an additional screening process

484
00:33:55,501 --> 00:33:57,640
in water-filled classifying tanks.

485
00:33:57,641 --> 00:34:05,140
much like panning for gold the finer sand particles rise to the top of the water separator

486
00:34:05,141 --> 00:34:12,080
and the heavier ones drop to the bottom this is the better sand that we pull out of our core sand

487
00:34:12,081 --> 00:34:17,820
we then take this material let it go by gravity back down to the ground level pump it back up

488
00:34:17,821 --> 00:34:23,740
again and resize it even further after processing the sand and gravel are ready to be shipped

489
00:34:23,741 --> 00:34:30,300
The construction aggregate business is so competitive that shipping costs are a major concern.

490
00:34:31,820 --> 00:34:35,940
Therefore, most quarries are located close to construction site areas.

491
00:34:36,840 --> 00:34:41,260
Construction aggregates are typically used within 20 to 30 miles of their point of production.

492
00:34:42,140 --> 00:34:44,840
That is, unless there are no local suppliers.

493
00:34:45,300 --> 00:34:47,940
Then the material will have to be shipped longer distances.

494
00:34:49,940 --> 00:34:53,480
In this case, a barge is the likely transport.

495
00:34:53,740 --> 00:34:56,740
This pit is adjacent to the Appomattox River.

496
00:34:56,741 --> 00:34:58,740
We ship quite a bit of our material on barges

497
00:34:58,741 --> 00:35:00,740
down to the Norfolk area.

498
00:35:00,741 --> 00:35:04,740
Finished product is loaded on our barge loadout facility

499
00:35:04,741 --> 00:35:05,740
here behind me.

500
00:35:05,741 --> 00:35:08,740
Dump trucks dumping in a grizzly hopper

501
00:35:08,741 --> 00:35:10,740
up the conveyor belt onto the barge.

502
00:35:10,741 --> 00:35:13,740
This barge will hold approximately 2,000 tons.

503
00:35:15,740 --> 00:35:18,740
The aggregate is often shipped to concrete plants

504
00:35:18,741 --> 00:35:22,740
and then sent off to make our churches, swimming pools,

505
00:35:22,741 --> 00:35:24,980
swimming pools, and shopping malls.

506
00:35:27,940 --> 00:35:30,540
Although rocks are most useful as building materials,

507
00:35:31,440 --> 00:35:35,520
they may soon rock our world in a surprising new way.

508
00:35:35,521 --> 00:35:38,560
In fact, we're starting to light up our cities

509
00:35:38,561 --> 00:35:42,200
with an endless power source not so deep beneath our feet.

510
00:35:44,700 --> 00:35:46,460
The world is looking for sources

511
00:35:46,461 --> 00:35:49,260
of clean, reliable, and renewable energy.

512
00:35:49,261 --> 00:35:52,360
Northern California has found it.

513
00:35:52,960 --> 00:35:56,560
We're in the Mayocamas Mountains of California's coast range

514
00:35:56,561 --> 00:35:58,520
at the Geysers Power Plants.

515
00:35:59,260 --> 00:36:02,160
The Geysers Power Plants are geothermal power plants

516
00:36:02,161 --> 00:36:04,680
that cover 40 square miles of the mountains here

517
00:36:04,681 --> 00:36:10,080
and generate enough electricity to provide 850,000 households with electric power.

518
00:36:10,960 --> 00:36:15,060
It's the largest geothermal area's force-producing power in the world.

519
00:36:16,280 --> 00:36:19,000
And where does this geothermal energy come from?

520
00:36:19,260 --> 00:36:20,960
Hot rocks.

521
00:36:21,860 --> 00:36:28,300
In most places, molten rock or magma exists very deep in the Earth, where temperatures are extremely high.

522
00:36:29,640 --> 00:36:34,340
The geysers area is unique in that the magma is very close to the Earth's surface.

523
00:36:34,341 --> 00:36:40,800
The heat that supplies the geysers is supplied from liquid magma about five miles deep.

524
00:36:41,060 --> 00:36:46,420
This liquid magma was left over from a volcanic period that existed here about 1.3 million years ago.

525
00:36:46,421 --> 00:36:49,900
That volcanism is long since gone, but it's left behind these pools of magma.

526
00:36:51,700 --> 00:36:55,260
In some of the geysers, this heat bubbles right up to the surface.

527
00:36:56,000 --> 00:36:58,520
This is a steam vent, also known as a fumarole,

528
00:36:58,680 --> 00:37:01,760
and it's evidence that we're very close to a geothermal resource here.

529
00:37:02,080 --> 00:37:05,640
Steam exits that vent at 250 degrees, causing that water to boil.

530
00:37:06,060 --> 00:37:08,040
If you fell in there, you could get boiled alive.

531
00:37:09,860 --> 00:37:14,380
The owners of the Calpine Corporation operate most of the geyser power plants,

532
00:37:14,381 --> 00:37:17,980
but they aren't the first ones to take advantage of this unique place.

533
00:37:18,800 --> 00:37:22,120
This area was known to the Indians thousands of years ago when they lived here.

534
00:37:22,240 --> 00:37:25,140
They utilized the hot springs for hot steam and for hot water.

535
00:37:26,000 --> 00:37:30,760
Later on in about 1847, an explorer named William Bell Elliott happened on this area

536
00:37:30,761 --> 00:37:34,080
and was really quite surprised to see steam venting out of the ground

537
00:37:34,081 --> 00:37:35,640
and hot bubbling mud coming up.

538
00:37:36,160 --> 00:37:39,480
And so he returned to his companions exclaiming that he'd found the gates of hell.

539
00:37:39,481 --> 00:37:47,720
During the 1920s, several attempts were made to tap the geothermal energy resources here for electrical power.

540
00:37:48,600 --> 00:37:55,720
But it wasn't until the 1950s that drilling technology became advanced enough to make the resource truly productive.

541
00:37:56,860 --> 00:38:03,280
They started drilling thermal wells deep into the Earth's core to capture the steam and utilize it to generate power.

542
00:38:03,281 --> 00:38:06,320
From there, in the 60s, the first plant was built,

543
00:38:06,720 --> 00:38:09,580
and since then, they've built up to 23 plants

544
00:38:09,581 --> 00:38:12,400
that have operated up here in the geysers almost for 50 years.

545
00:38:13,500 --> 00:38:17,460
The wells at the geysers don't have to reach all the way down to the liquid magma,

546
00:38:17,700 --> 00:38:19,840
but only to where the rocks are hot enough

547
00:38:19,841 --> 00:38:23,040
and there is enough water to create a large supply of steam.

548
00:38:25,920 --> 00:38:30,120
Nevertheless, many of the wells are drilled over two miles into the ground

549
00:38:30,121 --> 00:38:31,860
until they reach sandstone.

550
00:38:31,861 --> 00:38:35,060
The sandstone's been heated and it has water in it that turned into steam.

551
00:38:35,460 --> 00:38:37,940
But on top of that sandstone is what's called a cap rock.

552
00:38:38,160 --> 00:38:41,720
And that cap rock holds all that heat and steam pressure down in the rock.

553
00:38:42,160 --> 00:38:45,560
We drill down through that cap rock and into what we call a geotermal reservoir.

554
00:38:45,960 --> 00:38:49,100
And that reservoir is highly fractured so that there's cracks and fissures

555
00:38:49,101 --> 00:38:51,640
that allow that steam to travel essentially through the rock

556
00:38:51,641 --> 00:38:53,400
and then into our well-bore pipe.

557
00:38:54,240 --> 00:38:57,880
The drilling equipment is identical to that used for oil and gas wells.

558
00:38:57,881 --> 00:39:02,960
It's kind of drilling that we're in right now.

559
00:39:02,961 --> 00:39:05,260
We're in hard rock drilling, very deep.

560
00:39:05,261 --> 00:39:07,320
We use these tungsten carbide bits here.

561
00:39:07,321 --> 00:39:09,080
These are the cutting edges here.

562
00:39:09,081 --> 00:39:10,080
It's very, very hard.

563
00:39:10,081 --> 00:39:13,160
It wears very, very long time.

564
00:39:13,161 --> 00:39:17,820
Once drilled, the steam is channeled into an intricate network of pipelines, stretching

565
00:39:17,821 --> 00:39:20,460
over 100 miles.

566
00:39:20,461 --> 00:39:25,500
This is a geothermal wellhead connected to a steam well that extends two miles underground.

567
00:39:25,501 --> 00:39:30,340
The steam exits this well head at 350 degrees Fahrenheit and the steam is transported down

568
00:39:30,341 --> 00:39:34,040
the pipeline at 70 miles an hour to the power plant.

569
00:39:34,041 --> 00:39:38,740
The pipe itself originates from rock material, primarily iron ore.

570
00:39:38,741 --> 00:39:40,820
The pipe is made out of iron.

571
00:39:40,821 --> 00:39:43,220
When iron gets hot, it expands.

572
00:39:43,221 --> 00:39:47,740
When the iron starts out cold when it's first installed, it might only be 35, 40 degrees

573
00:39:47,741 --> 00:39:51,380
here at the geysers and this is 350 degrees right here.

574
00:39:51,381 --> 00:39:54,220
So the pipe has to be allowed to expand and contract.

575
00:39:54,221 --> 00:39:57,300
And that's why everything is mounted on these little shoes like this.

576
00:39:57,440 --> 00:40:00,660
It gives us some leeway when it gets hot and cold to slide back and forth.

577
00:40:01,580 --> 00:40:04,000
The steam is piped to giant turbines.

578
00:40:07,500 --> 00:40:10,020
This power is then transferred to a generator.

579
00:40:11,320 --> 00:40:14,880
That generator is generating 50 megawatts of electricity right now,

580
00:40:14,881 --> 00:40:16,980
and up to power 50,000 homes.

581
00:40:18,280 --> 00:40:21,620
The electricity is then sent all over northern California.

582
00:40:21,621 --> 00:40:27,560
Then, it's time for the water to pay another visit to the hot rocks.

583
00:40:27,561 --> 00:40:30,700
What you see behind me here is the power plant cooling tower.

584
00:40:30,701 --> 00:40:34,280
After the steam has expended its energy in the turbine, it's condensed and sent out here

585
00:40:34,281 --> 00:40:37,720
to the cooling tower to be cooled where it could be injected back into the ground to

586
00:40:37,721 --> 00:40:39,380
produce more steam.

587
00:40:39,381 --> 00:40:42,440
What you see coming out of the top of the cooling tower is not smoke, it's just pure

588
00:40:42,441 --> 00:40:46,480
water vapor that's being cooled through evaporation.

589
00:40:46,481 --> 00:40:50,520
We have an endless supply of energy in which we can generate power.

590
00:40:50,521 --> 00:40:53,680
This is renewable energy that works around the clock.

591
00:40:53,681 --> 00:40:59,800
The geothermal power comes up naturally 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.

592
00:40:59,801 --> 00:41:04,040
Across the globe, many countries are looking to the heat of hot rocks for future energy

593
00:41:04,041 --> 00:41:05,040
needs.

594
00:41:05,041 --> 00:41:07,880
The potential for geothermal energy is huge.

595
00:41:07,881 --> 00:41:11,080
The Earth has an inexhaustible supply of energy.

596
00:41:11,081 --> 00:41:16,280
Worldwide, geothermal energy is produced in about 20 different countries.

597
00:41:16,281 --> 00:41:21,900
In the areas of the world where steam isn't as close to the surface as it is at the geysers,

598
00:41:21,901 --> 00:41:26,660
engineers are experimenting with a process called hot dry rock technology.

599
00:41:26,661 --> 00:41:32,560
In hot dry rock geothermal technology, there's no steam locked up in the hot rock that exists

600
00:41:32,561 --> 00:41:34,040
down under the crust.

601
00:41:34,041 --> 00:41:39,280
So what engineers have tried to do is drill down into that rock and then taking whatever

602
00:41:39,281 --> 00:41:43,860
water source they happen to have available at the surface, pump it down into a well,

603
00:41:43,861 --> 00:41:47,700
work its way out into the cracks and fissures in that hot dry rock,

604
00:41:47,701 --> 00:41:50,360
and then drill more wells around the perimeter

605
00:41:50,361 --> 00:41:54,200
and try to recover that water as steam to produce electricity.

606
00:41:54,201 --> 00:41:57,900
The wells have to be deeper with hot rock technology,

607
00:41:57,901 --> 00:42:00,980
but theoretically, the process could produce enough energy

608
00:42:00,981 --> 00:42:05,020
to supply the entire world's demands.

609
00:42:05,021 --> 00:42:08,660
Not bad for a bunch of rocks.

610
00:42:08,661 --> 00:42:11,220
Whether they're creating energy for our homes,

611
00:42:11,221 --> 00:42:15,260
Iron for our industries or concrete for our infrastructure.

612
00:42:15,261 --> 00:42:18,820
Rocks partner with us in stony silence.

613
00:42:18,821 --> 00:42:21,060
They've stood by us in the past,

614
00:42:21,061 --> 00:42:23,560
and they will support our future.

615
00:42:23,561 --> 00:42:24,560
Rock on.

